Date: Fri, 17 Dec 93 14:50:11 PST
From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Subject: Game Theory is Fun!

Mike Price writes:
>How does Pavlov operate?  My understanding is that Pavlov starts out
>being nasty to see how tough you are (the opposite tack from tit-for-
>tat, which starts each interaction with a player by being friendly). 
>If you shoot back he backs down [cooperates], otherwise he pisses all
>over you [rips off suckers].  Is that a fair summary?  How does Pavlov
>square up against TFT?

There is so much to game theory that we didn't cover the iterated PD
much, and so I've never heard of Pavlov.  Btw, a Nature article last
year describes a competition where Win-Stay-Lose-Switch (WSLS) beats
all TFT versions and everything else.  WSLS is where you do what you
did last time if you got one of the two high payoffs, and switch if
you got one of the other two.  And in contrast to TFT, WSLS is
"subgame perfect" with itself, so that players would want to follow
the strategy no matter what happened.

Here's a fun game theory example.  Imagine two companies are competing
for some market, or competing to first get a patent in some area.
Each would rather the other quit, and at first each would rather just
quit if they knew the other would never quit.  However at some
"stay-anyway" point each has invested so much in the project that they
rather continue even if the other will never quit.  The only subgame
perfect equilibrium to this game has the guy who has the later
stay-anyway point quit on day one, assuming they both know what both
points are.  This is another reason why quickly getting to that
positive cash flow point is so important to new businesses.

Robin Hanson

