Of course, "survival" is not the important issue, evolutionarily
speaking. What counts is _differential reproduction_.

Which of the following two groups do you think will have more genetic
effect (albeit small) on the future of homo sapiens:

* Typical Extropians, who tend to be childless, or have relatively few
children. Even if they live to a ripe old age (or beyond).

or

* Typical Christian Fundamentalists (or Muslims, Catholics, Mormons,
whatever), who tend to have largish families. Even if they die at an
early age (after having a passal of kids).

Not that I think genetics is all that important on a time scale of
mere centuries (and centuries from now it'll be a whole new ball game,
no matter who's right about the arrival of nanotech and the
Singularity).

To the extent it matters at all, I suspect those who did the bidding
of the local authorities probably lived long enough to outreproduce
those who did their own thing and got shot full of arrows by the
Sheriff's men. Whether this tendency was a result of genetic factors
and thus got selected out or not I'll leave for the evolutionary
biologists to further debate.

Me, I care about _me_, not about my (alleged) nanocontribution to the
gene pool.

--Tim May


