Subject: NEURO: drugs and memory

At  9:12 AM 12/17/93 -0800, chip@netcom.com (Chip Morningstar -- "Software
Without Moving P wrote:
>My short term memory is pretty bad even without chemical stimulation.  This
>thread prompts me to ask if anyone knows of things which have the opposite
>effect, i.e., enhance short term memory.  Seems plausible to me but none of
>the smart drug literature I've read seems to address this directly.

Do you mean short-term enhancement of short-term memory, or long-term
enhancement of short-term memory?  For STST, any CNS stimulant -- caffeine,
ephedrine, phenylalanine, or harder stuff -- will do.

For LTST, I recommend hydergine and vasopressin, based on my own
(admittedly anecdotal) data.  It's important to note that the STST
stimulants mentioned above *reduce* LTST because the body becomes
habituated to them... after a while, if you _don't_ get that morning
caffeine, you're stupider than baseline.  My advice is to cut out the
STST's (if you use any on a regular basis... remember that phenylalanine is
in Nutrasweet) and save them for when you really _need_ them, while
starting in on hydergine and vasopressin.
