In the age of ::excludes, the list "justice" mechanism is no
longer necessary.  I resent the exclusion of posters that I
would not have excluded myself (Tim May, Dave Krieger, and
Mike Price most recently).  If you find that someone usually
offends you, ::exclude them.  If someone flames you, or if
you think someone was overly brusque, send them private mail.
If a thread has degenerated into a flamewar, exclude the 
thread.  Lefty suggested a FLAME: prefix, if you must flame,
prefix it so that others may filter it.

I vividly recall the recent cases where Tim May and Dave Krieger
were banned for posting "flames" that were actually posts I
enjoyed reading.  If someone is a persistent problem, like Bruce 
"Pandit Singh" White, or Dale Worley, then they should simply
be removed.  A note from Harry such as "I have deleted Pandit
Singh, because I thought his continued presence detrimental 
to list quality" would be more than enough.  An entire legal
system is unnecessary for the management of a mailing list.

Let's drop the elaborate legal pretenses, the charges based on 
hurt, statutes of limitations, ppl list adjudication, 3rd circuit
appeal courts, etc.  

  Harry writes:  
  The changes I am thinking of are:
  1) A new adjudicator (Andrea resigned)
  2) A new judge
  3) I become the list DA. I can bring charges before the judge.
  4) Anyone else can bring charges; If i don't like your "charge
  I will tell you to file it yourself)

Your own funding of the list software has obviated the system
of charges.  My thanks go to you and Ray for delivering the 
coup de grace to what was a severely flawed system.

  5) Since no one has started a competitive legal system I assume
  "ours" is not so bad; I don't feel the barriers to entry are that
  high (maybe 20 hours per year?)
  
I can only interpret this as evidence that no such system is 
necessary.  Not even the original.  

We've travelled too far up this legalistic spiral, give anarchy 
a chance.  

