hope that you subscribe or find a copy at your local newsstand.

The unifying characteristic of the list recipients is their interest
in libertarian politics, techniques of life extension (including
cryonics), technological extension of human intelligence and
perception, nanotechnology, spontaneous orders, memetics, and a number
of other related ideas. We are also interested in the relationship
among the previous ideas. If these topics seem to you to be naturally
related and mutually consistent, you may already be an Extropian.

This list is private. Your continued subscription indicates your
acceptance of our rules. In Extropian terms, this list is governed by
a poly-centric legal code, or a Privately Produced Law (PPL). While
having such detailed rules may seem odd or even strange to you, the
code/rules are used here for three reasons.

1) We believe in testing Extropian theory. The use of a poly-centric
legal code for this list is such a test.

2) Various Extropians place different property values on what they
write.  Some wish to retain all rights to the material; others wish to
make their posts freely available. The rules will hopefully insure
that everyone's material will be treated in the manner they desire.

3) Time is valuable. To keep many people active and reading the list,
we respect them by keeping the signal-to-noise ratio as high as
possible.

We suggested you read the postings for a while before you begin to
post. In that way you will have a better idea of how the list works.
Also, topics vary from week to week, and month to month; sometimes we
are very technical, other times very political, and other times very
trival.  Your first impression of the list will be influenced by
current topics and issues of the day. At times only ten or twenty
people actively post, while over 300 people actively read the list. We
discourage new members from making a lot of short posts. We do
encourage new members to post after they have 1) got a feel for the
flavor of the list, 2) have something that adds to the over-all
discussion.

Please note, again, that communication to the Extropian mailing list
is *private*. Nothing you receive from the list may be forwarded to
third parties without explicit permission from the author. Each reader
of the list must have an active subscription or be registered with the
list administrator (e.g., you can register your spouse so you can
share messages). You are welcome to keep archival copies of list
traffic you receive for personal use only.

1) Requests and complaints

 1a) Formal complaints and administrative requests, as well as requests
     to join or discontinue the list, Must be sent to:

               Extropians-request@extropy.org


 1b) Please allow up to 5 business days for your requests to be
processed.  Please note that most requests are handled as soon as they
are read. The handling of requests on the days just prior to, during
and after holidays will be greatly delayed.

2) Posting to the list.

 2a) Mail to the list should be sent to:

              extropians@extropy.org

 2b) The software that processes traffic on the list alters the mail
headers so that all correctly working mail readers will automatically
address replies BACK to the LIST. [reply-to: extropians@extropy.org]

 2c) If you want to reply to an individual poster you should manually
address a reply. If the post does not have a signature line, viewing
the message in a text editor should help you determine the sender's
address.  (See the software commands ::reply and ::private.)

 2d) It is strongly recommend but not required that all list members
use a signature file or an x-header containing their e-mail address.

 3) Quoting other posts: Due to the volume of list traffic and the
cost of disk storage, we ask to you restrain yourself from
over-quoting previous posts -- just a couple of lines to re-cap for
those who weren't paying attention is usually sufficient. Since any
recent post can be retrived from our database, anyone who is
interested can obtain the message you are replying to -- do not
over-quote.

4) Purpose:

 4a) We created teh list as a forum for the exchange of new
information and techniques, and not as a forum for debating the
basics. We do have our disagreements -- often quite lively ones -- but
rarely about really basic issues.  For example, arguments in favor of
socialized medicine or dying a natural death at age sixty are, judging
by past experience, likely to be refuted, and finally ignored. If you
insist upon making such posts you will most likely be removed from the
list. Many list member enjoy such debates and will be happy to do so
in the appropriate places like alt.talk.politics.

 4b) Because a few former list members have violated rule 4, members
often refer new members to the suggested reading list. If you find
that you are being referred to the reading list please take that
suggestion strongly. Many members of the list have thought about and
discussed these ideas for many many years. While it is possible that
you have found some unique "basic" objection to our views, it is
unlikely. We strongly encourage posts that refine our ideas by
bringing new prospectives; we remove users from the list who
repeatedly question the basics.

5) Subject prefixes:

 5a) Posts about the list, or its rules, MUST have the prefix META:
All other posts should have a prefix indicating their contents
examples include, PHIL:, MATH:, SCI:, CHAT:, etc.

Posts containing messages forwarded to the list MUST have use the 
FORWARD: or FWD: prefix.

 5b) Several times a year ExI may hold on-line pledge drives, these
posts will have the PLEDGE: prefix.

 5c) Polls being submitted to list members will have the POLL: prefix.
 
Usage of META, FORWARD, POLL, and PLEDGE are required. All other
prefixes are voluntary.

 5d) Use your imagination and define your own meaningful prefixes.

6) Etiquette:

 6a) Traffic on this mailing list can run quite high, sometimes more
than 100 messages per day.  We like to keep the signal-to-noise ratio
as high as we can, so please restrain the impulse to post "me-too"
messages or ad hominem flames.  If you cannot resist engaging in such
discourse, please do so in private e-mail.

 6b) Fighting and insulting are NOT allowed! We interpret these
rules rather strictly. What may pass as heated debate in other forums
is not allowed here.

 6c) If someone starts a fight with you what should you do ?

We have a private legal code set-up to handle such disputes which
includes a judge ( the list administrator) and a adjudicator to handle
appeals.  Because the list administrator doesn't wish to both file
charges and then judge them, in almost all cases list members MUST
bring the charges.

PPL's differ from other legal codes because they get better as they
are used. Traditional legal codes get bogged down in precedence and
common law which date back to the early ages of man. PPL's can prune
un-wanted precedence and alter themselves to better serve their users.
Don't be afraid to make charges because you are afraid of being a
"tattle tale." If you are unsure about bringing charges discuss them
with the list administrator. When charges are filed everyone learns
including the judge; through repeated trials and errors we will
develop a community that we all will enjoy living in.

If someone starts a fight, you may do any of the following:
1) Nothing - give no response 
2) Respond privately
3) Make a formal complaint to the list administrator asking for a 
judgment against that person.

Note: Do not post charges to the list; present your charges directly
to the list administrator

 6d) What if you just have to respond to 
1) "uphold your honor"?  
2) set the "facts/record straight" ?  
3) tell the entire list why you will not be speaking or reading posts
or messages from the person who offended you?

    D O N ' T

We consider such responses flames in their own right, and you would be
taking the law into your own hands; you might also be censured or
removed from the list.

  Remember: Don't contribute to or continue a fight once one has
started.  Doing so places you in violation of the list rules. That
includes meta communication, which includes posts with an angry tone.

7) Questions: Don't be afraid to ask questions.  This is an open forum
for the interchange of information about Extropian topics... speak up!
Many of the answers can be found in the books listed at the end of
